 "implications": {
  "jipoe": "Analyst assessment derived from cited sources; validate before use. STEP 1: Include state and local government systems and commercial software suppliers in the OE. They support National Guard, emergency management and DoD-adjacent functions and are soft entry points. STEP 2: Abuse of legitimate cloud services for C2 blends with normal business traffic. STEP 3: The template is web-application exploitation or spear-phishing, then web shells and custom backdoors, then credential theft and lateral movement, plus theft of code-signing material for later supply-chain use. Re-compromise after eviction is likely. STEP 4: MLCOA is continued espionage against government and technology targets mixed with profit-driven theft. MDCOA is a supply-chain operation using stolen signing certificates to reach DoD or DIB networks during a crisis. Candidate indicators: .NET deserialization exploit attempts, newly signed binaries with unexpected certificates, and calendar or cloud-API traffic from servers [S1][S2][S3].",
  "jcc2": "Analyst assessment derived from cited sources; validate before use. (1) Expect re-entry after eviction: JCC2 tracking should keep cases open with heightened monitoring after remediation. (2) Coordinate with state partners (National Guard cyber units, state CISOs, CISA) because state networks are in the target set. (3) Monitor code-signing certificate revocations and abuse reports as part of supply-chain situational awareness. (4) Cloud-service C2 requires provider cooperation (e.g. Google, Microsoft) for disruption, which should be built into response playbooks."
